my it  might be very difficult to assume the  upright posture and engage those  abdominal muscles when you've had a  laparotomy  maybe you might see something a little  bit different if the source of  discomfort will say a craniotomy  or a paw so perhaps  grooming transfer gets more to a dynamic  pain status which is  uh it hurts when i do this versus  more of a static pain status which is it  hurts all the time even when i'm sitting  still  so nest consolidation and grooming  transfer together might give us a really  nice  picture of both static and dynamic pain  acute early pain and more longer lasting  pain  only minor differences were noted when  we looked across all of our different  sources of variation  whether it was sex inbred outbread we  did notice that grooming transfer can be  used in socially housed mice  and that having an existing nest doesn't  interfere with the ability to assess  nest consolidation after surgery  when we look overall at our pain effects  again we're seeing  differences out to about 12 hours for  nest consolidation and out to about 48  hours with grooming transfer  so let's look at our analgesic drugs we  have our saline treated animals which  had  decreases in their nest consolidation  scores out through 12 hours  and when they were treated with uh  buprenorphine there was only a single  difference there  uh carprophen a slightly later time  point at four hours  happily with the multimodal treated  animals had both buprenorphine and  carpropin  we saw no delays and so we were able to  completely control for  pain associated decreases in nest  consolidation scores  when we look at grooming transfer scores  so sailing treated as an  a we can see that there were many time  points  2 and then 12 through 48 where we saw  decreases in grooming transfer with  saline treated animals  we saw slightly fewer with buprenorphine  although still present at 12  24 and 36 and carprophen about the same  as with saline  and multimodal about the same as with  buprenorphine so  still some room to improve analgesic  efficacy according to the grooming  transfer test  so looking across all the broad spectra  of different tests that we ran  with saline we had decreased body weight  at 24 and 48 hours so as a clinical  measure it was able to recognize  animals that underwent surgery but did  not receive any analgesics  for nest consolidation scores we saw  decreases at 2 4 8 and 12.  for grooming transfer we had decreases  in grooming behavior out through 48  hours  a single time point where unalleviated  pain resulted in a decrease in  mechanical threshold  and two time points at which local motor  activity was depressed  however if we had only looked at body  weight mechanical threshold and local  motor activity we would have missed some  really interesting changes  so for example with carbophene we're  able to see that there are  no decreases between those three  assessments  and there is some improvement in nest  consolidation because we only have a  decrease at one time point  and grooming transfer interestingly um  the only difference we really saw was  that  the pain appeared to be alleviated at 48  hours versus 36.  with buprenorphine we saw even fewer  differences between our surgery and  anesthesia group showing us that pain  was even more well controlled and with  multimodal  we had no differences in nested consol  nest consolidation  so it was like they had never had the  surgery and then grooming transfer we  saw  very few differences at those three time  points and again that's more of a  dynamic pain it hurts when i do this  so i think it's good that we're  controlling for that static pain which  is even if you're just resting  their pain levels were controlled  overall for analgesia efficacy  multimodal appeared to have  the best control of post laparotomy pain  and mice  followed by buprenorphine carprophen  which again was about 30 mix per keg  in the water uh was much delayed and  not nearly as good and then saline um  was only slightly better or slightly  worse than the carboprofen  for conclusions we were happy to find  that nest consolidation and grooming  transfer tests  appeared to be both valid measures for  post-op pain  they were fast easy to use can be used  cage side  and they can be used in a diverse array  of situations whether mice were  male female inbred outbred socially  housed singly housed  with or without an existing nest and so  hopefully can be applied to  the wide array of situations that you  find in your standard mouse liberia  multimodal analgesia appeared to have  the most efficacious post-op pain  although there was some partial coverage  noted with the grooming transfer test so  certainly it would be good to look into  this more and see if we could play  around with  different doses or some different  analgesics  carproof in the water unfortunately was  not sufficient as a single agent  analgesic  and this sort of falls in line with  other publications that have found  things  such as acetaminophen to be insufficient  for post laparotomy pain and mice  future directions we would love to be  able to um  titrate some of these to figure out if  there's a threshold for  treatment so if you see a score of x and  it should be y  that you should definitely pull the  trigger and provide some additional  analgesics  we'd like to be able to evaluate  additional analgesic regimens and see if  we can find more of a sweet spot  where we're able to control for pain  we'd like to look at other surgery sites  as i mentioned  the grooming transfer test in particular  it might depend on  where the site of the surgery or the  pain is so we did a laparotomy which is  pain  from the abdomen you might see something  very different if the source of pain is  the head or the paw or thoracotomy  meaning the chest wall it would be  really cool to see if this works with  other rodents such as rats because they  can also be really challenging to assess  post-op and it might have applications  beyond just looking at post-surgical  pain  it might be that we can use it to assess  end-of-life care decisions and human  endpoints  so with that i'd like to thank dr  vanessa oliver again who did  a large amount of this work and we could  not have been sharing these results  without her  our refinement and enrichment  advancements laboratory at the  university of michigan  with our lead technician sarah thurston  observers who helped us assess  these tests cage side jenny jones katie  simon dr kimberly jen  doctors lucy kennedy and dr sarah martin  and our funding  came generously from the grants for  laboratory animal science or glass  awards  um which are sponsored by alas as well  as dr bob disco in the unit for  laboratory animal medicine  and with that i'll take some questions  thank you dr lofgren for that  informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question drop  down box located on the far  left of your presentation window type  your question into the box that appears  on your screen  and click the send button once there as  many of your questions as we have time  for  now let's get started so our first  question is  dr lochran can you provide some  additional details on how you chose the  car profin water dose  yes thank you that's an excellent  question um we were definitely we're  looking to provide  some type of analgesic that wouldn't  require injections and i know delivering  analgesics in the water is something  that we often get asked about  and so this is something we decided to  explore um  we knew that from a publication in jlass  in 2013  they had already found that mice would  readily drink carpropin in the water  though not so for maloxicam there must  be something about the taste the mice  weren't keen on it but  they would readily drink the carproven  they actually tended to drink it  more than the water so we were pretty  enthusiastic that that might be a good  option  we then conducted a pilot study to  evaluate the 10 mg per kig dose that was  described in this j  last paper um and what we found  was uh here we have water consumption on  the y-axis  we have time on the x-axis regular water  consumption is in black  and in green is the carbon water so  these are the animals that  um would undergo surgery and drink  coffin water  and we found that the animals were just  as likely to be able to drink  the carproof and water in fact they  actually drank more of it  as compared to regular water at several  different time points  so at least that gave us full faith that  they would consume it after surgery um  and then we did pk analysis so we  collected blood spun it down for plasma  and sent it out to some of our  colleagues at the university of colorado  and what they found was the plasma  concentration remained  high the whole time that the animals  were on the carproof water so  they received it 24 hours as their  standard drinking water in a water  bottle  before they had any measures taken and  then time zero then  they had had it for 24 hours and we kept  the water bottle on for 48 hours  and we also pulled a sample at 72 hours  to see what would happen once you took  the car broken bottle  away and just gave them standard water  so you can see that the levels were up  around  30 micrograms per milliliter and based  on the literature  um that i think is derived from dogs  this was thought to be  an adequate efficacious um plasma  concentration for this drug  so we hypothesized that it was really  reaching therapeutic thresholds  but to test this we ran it through a  number of the same tests that i just  went through including mechanical  threshold nest consolidation and  grooming  and had some concerns so i'll discuss  those briefly um in that pilot test  where we provided the 10  10 migs per kg of carbon uh for nest  consolidation scores we found at  baseline they had scores of about four  between two and 24 hours um after  surgery those scores were significantly  depressed  for the first 12 hours and so we had  some concerns that analgesia wasn't  being met  when we looked at grooming transfer  scores here um you can see the black  line baseline  so um good grooming transfer scores and  reaching maximal by 24 to 36  hours but after surgery we had  significant delays  at several time points and so again had  some concerns that maybe analgesia  wasn't where we wanted it to be  and then when we did mechanical  thresholds with the von frey  we saw two time points at which there  was a decreased threshold  so the animals were more sensitive to  mechanical  stimuli after surgery than they were  after baseline  so even though they would readily drink  the 10 meg per keg  and we thought the therapeutic level had  been reached in the steady state  it didn't show the analgesia efficacy  that we were hoping for  and so we went into the literature again  and looked at some  other papers that had similarly been  disappointed with  carboprofin at standard doses and chose  a higher dose  30 minutes per keg to assess so these  are the volumes of mice sorry volumes of  carbon water  that were consumed by the mice on our  full study  and you can see here um the black bar is  the car profin  uh water the multimodal or the animal  side carproof and water  plus uh injections of buprenorphine so  um they were on par  and and readily drank the water in both  scenarios  and they had increases in water  consumption as compared to baseline so  again they were drinking it  more than they would drink their  standard water  we didn't see any differences in the  amount of water consumed  across any of our treatment groups or  whether or not they had had just  anesthesia  or they had had a surgery so they  weren't drinking more  say after having a painful condition and  self-treating  we did have some concerns when we  started looking at some of the blood  parameters  so not so much with  our cbc but more of their chemistry  we had some increases in creatinine and  ast  and i think that's because they were  actually consuming uh  potentially a higher dose than even the  30 per keg  because they really liked the water so i  think  caution is to be made when you're trying  to use  any sort of analgesic in the water and  that you need to make sure the animals  are consuming it  that published pk values are helpful but  don't necessarily  guarantee analgesia efficacy so it's  important not only to have the plasma  levels but also to do  the analgesia testing to make sure that  those plasma levels  correlate to reduced pain and then  with nsaids as it turned out with  carproof and if the mice really like  drinking coconut water  perhaps it tastes sweet they're going to  drink it and  potentially get a higher dose than you  intend them to and there might be  some possible sequelae for different  organ functions  and even at 30 minutes per cake we  didn't necessarily see the efficacy that  we wanted to see so  i think at this time i wouldn't  recommend carproof and water  as at least a soul analgesic but for  future directions it would be great to  look at  a lower dose and see if we could pair it  with  something like an opiate to have good  analgesia efficacy without the risk of  potentially having  some off-target effects thanks
 

